; =====================================================================
; embedded-abnf-dedented.derived.abnf
;
; DERIVED -- NOT verbatim.  This is this library's rendering, not RFC 9165's.
; Do not cite it as the standard's text; cite embedded-abnf.abnf, which holds
; the source's own bytes.
;
; WHAT IT IS.  The four ABNF controller strings a tool must actually parse
; when it evaluates Figures 3, 4 and 5 - that is, the result of applying the
; document's own operators.  Each block below is produced by applying
; Section 2.3's definition of dedenting, LITERALLY AS WRITTEN:
;
;      1.  determining the smallest amount of leftmost blank space (number
;          of leading space characters) present in all the non-blank lines,
;          and
;      2.  removing exactly that number of leading space characters from
;          each line.  For blank (blank space only or empty) lines, there
;          may be fewer (or no) leading space characters than this amount,
;          in which case all leading space is removed.
;
; and then concatenating per Section 2.2.  Nothing else is done: no rule is
; reordered, renamed, added or removed.
;
; DEDENTING IS MARGIN-INVARIANT, which is why this works at all.  OURS: the
; RFC prints each figure shifted right by its body margin, so every line of a
; literal shifts by the same amount and step 1's minimum shifts with it.
; Dedenting the margin-bearing form and dedenting the author's flush-left form
; therefore give the same bytes.  This is what lets the figures in a published
; RFC be correct examples rather than approximations.
;
; CLOSURE, CHECKED (OURS, by script, from the cache):
;   cbor-tags-oid      4 rules, 0 undefined.  CLOSED from `oid`; CLOSED from
;                      `roid`.  3 of the 4 reachable from each selector.
;   rfc3339 .det core  14 rules (13 + DIGIT), 0 undefined.  CLOSED from
;                      `full-date` (5 of the 14 reachable: full-date,
;                      date-fullyear, date-month, date-mday, DIGIT); CLOSED
;                      from `date-time` (all 14 reachable).  Both counts
;                      include the selector; pass 1 counted the first
;                      exclusively at 4 and the second inclusively at 14.
;   So both ABNF operator controller strings in this document satisfy
;   Section 3's own closure rule (rfc-9165#R-0055, "all rules referenced need
;   to be defined in each ABNF operator controller string"), INCLUDING across
;   the .det composition.  The abbreviation "; abbreviated here" in
;   rfc5234-core does not break closure, because nothing in rfc3339 references
;   any core rule other than DIGIT.
;
; CASE SENSITIVITY, CHECKED (OURS): the only quoted strings containing letters
; anywhere in this ABNF are "T" and "Z", in date-time and time-offset, and
; NO RFC 7405 %s or %i prefix appears.  RFC 5234 Section 2.3 makes quoted
; strings CASE INSENSITIVE, so `Tag0 = #6.0(text .abnf date-time)` admits
; `1990-12-31t23:59:60z`.  See README.md - this is how embedded ABNF gives
; CDDL a case-insensitivity that RFC 8610 Section 3.1 says the language does
; not have.
;   [correction] 2026-10-20 (cross-check pass): THE CENSUS IS VERIFIED, THE
;   PREMISE IS TESTIMONY.  The census above is measured from RFC 9165's own
;   cached bytes.  The rule it is applied to - that a quoted string is case
;   insensitive unless RFC 7405's %s says otherwise - comes from RFC 5234 and
;   RFC 7405, and this library holds NO record and NO cache of either.  The
;   acceptance of `1990-12-31t23:59:60z` was executed, but by a recogniser
;   whose literal matching is case-insensitive BY CONSTRUCTION, so the run
;   confirms the census and the consequence and cannot confirm the premise.
;   Ingesting RFC 5234 and RFC 7405 is what would close it.
;
; Source digest (sha256 of the full cached source text):
;   94f86116c04fde0f11d54576465181090ec33983d78db82527c0703a41976774
; Source: RFC 9165, "Additional Control Operators for the Concise
;         Data Definition Language (CDDL)", C. Bormann, December
;         2021, Standards Track, ISSN 2070-1721.
;         <https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9165.txt>
; =====================================================================

; ---- DERIVED D-1: Section 3's newline relaxation, dedented ----
; The document's own production.  Dedenting changes nothing but the margin.
CRLF = %x0A / %x0D.0A

; ---- DERIVED D-2: the controller of `"oid" .det cbor-tags-oid` ----
; = Figure 4's byte-string literal content, which the document states is
; equivalent: "For the first rule in Figure 3, the result is equivalent to
; Figure 4."  Line 1 is the Section 3 `element` selector; the rest is the
; rule-list.  The `roid` controller is identical but for the selector.
oid
oid = 1*arc
roid = *arc
arc = [nlsb] %x00-7f
nlsb = %x81-ff *%x80-ff

; ---- DERIVED D-3: the controller of `"date-time" .cat rfc3339` ----
; where `rfc3339 = '<A-3>' .det rfc5234-core`.  Line 1 is the selector;
; the blank line before DIGIT is the join of A-3's trailing newline and
; A-4's leading newline and is ABNF-legal.  The `full-date` controller is
; identical but for the selector.
date-time
date-fullyear   = 4DIGIT
date-month      = 2DIGIT  ; 01-12
date-mday       = 2DIGIT  ; 01-28, 01-29, 01-30, 01-31 based on
                          ; month/year
time-hour       = 2DIGIT  ; 00-23
time-minute     = 2DIGIT  ; 00-59
time-second     = 2DIGIT  ; 00-58, 00-59, 00-60 based on leap sec
                          ; rules
time-secfrac    = "." 1*DIGIT
time-numoffset  = ("+" / "-") time-hour ":" time-minute
time-offset     = "Z" / time-numoffset

partial-time    = time-hour ":" time-minute ":" time-second
                  [time-secfrac]
full-date       = date-fullyear "-" date-month "-" date-mday
full-time       = partial-time time-offset

date-time       = full-date "T" full-time

DIGIT          =  %x30-39 ; 0-9
; abbreviated here
